A persistent `{{ .Shell }}` terminal. `command` is what you type: at the prompt it is a command line and runs (Enter implied); while a program is running, answers and keystrokes go to that program, so write `\n` or `<enter>` where a person would press it. Named keys go in angle brackets: `<escape>`, `<tab>`, `<backspace>`, `<delete>`, `<up>`/`<down>`/`<left>`/`<right>`, `<home>`, `<end>`, `<pageup>`, `<pagedown>`, `<f1>`–`<f12>`, `<ctrl+c>` or any `<ctrl+letter>`; anything else in brackets is typed literally.

Sessions are independent terminals, one per `session` name (default `main`): state, working directory and running programs are per session. Keep long-lived programs - dev servers, watchers - in their own named session and work freely in another; a command typed while a session's silent command runs is queued and runs at the next prompt (send answers to a waiting program with an explicit `<enter>` instead). Polling a session streams a running command's output in chunks and delivers its exit code when it finishes; never spend a call on a bare `sleep` - it is refused: an empty call already waits for the next event, and waiting on a condition belongs in the command that checks it (a watch or a poll loop). A call returns when the process exits (with its exit code), stops for input, takes over the screen (a rendered {{ .DefaultCols }}x{{ .DefaultRows }} frame instead of a stream), or goes idle. An empty call waits for the next of those. A session whose shell has exited reports its final output and exit code on the next call to it and then opens a fresh shell in its original directory - none of the old state carries over. `reset` kills the named session and opens a fresh one, losing its state; last resort.

cwd, variables, activated environments and held credentials carry across calls within a session; a new session opens in the directory Harness was spawned from unless `working_dir` says otherwise. A result ends with `<cwd>…</cwd>` only when the shell moved. Output over {{ .MaxOutputLength }} characters keeps a head and a long tail.
